  "holdout_metric": null,
  "surprise": "Keep: 676 (682, 680, 667) against 702's 548 (535, 562, 546), up 105-145 on every seed; the first attempt's two surviving seeds scored 640 and 684 (its seed-1 pod had no working CUDA device, pretrained on CPU for 8 steps and timed out, so the whole experiment was re-run under the same registration; the first attempt is archived in artifacts/autoresearch_thoughts/previous_attempts). But the thoughts are not what scored. metric_diag at 0 thoughts is 670 and 656 on seeds 0 and 1, within 12-24 of the 8-thought score, and pretraining val loss is flat in the thought count on every seed (8 against 0 within 0.001). Seed 2's 121 at 0 thoughts is the no-thought path going out of distribution under RL that trains at 8 only (702's 329 came by the same route), not computation in the slots, since the identical recipe left seeds 0 and 1 level. So the spike's finding holds per line too: pause slots go unused, and the fed-back trigger state of 701-703 was what made 8 thoughts beat 0 there. The gain is speed. Single-pass training gave 3,398-3,784 pretraining steps (702: 1,530-1,651) and fine-tunes of 76-118 s instead of 195-254 s, so RL ran 6-7 rounds instead of 3 (RL targets 1,184 against 956) with 1,564-1,729 of 1,800 s charged. Two predictions failed: the pretraining val loss did not fall (0.0843-0.0869 against 702's 0.0817-0.0844 at 2.2x the steps, though held-out greedy accuracy rose to 0.953-0.963 from 0.899-0.920), so per step the pause model learns less than the feedback model; and the thoughts' contribution did not survive without feedback. Round-1 sample accuracy at 8 thoughts varied 5.4-18.9% across seeds (first attempt 2.6-5.2%), so pretrained-model variance stays large. Next: put the feedback back on top of this single-pass trunk at a cost below one pass per line (thought inputs from a lower block's state at the trigger, or one extra pass over the expanded sequence), and read whether 8 beats 0 in the pretraining val loss, the cheap place to see it.",
